Z||000000||DavidHenshaw||11-23-2000||01:14 PM||||Is it possible to adjust the fuel gage. Mine shows 1/4 tank left when it is empty.<P>Dave<P>86 GT 2.8||||reg||1
Z||000001||Sndfr||11-23-2000||01:37 PM||||i have a similar problem except mine is at quarter tank when FULL!<P>------------------<BR>-matthew d.<BR>"who's yo daddy?"||||reg||1
Z||000002||86se/lambo||11-24-2000||09:05 PM||||yes, but it's a rather tedious process. First you have to drop the tank and then with the sending unit/pump still connected <BR>adjust the two screws on the unit. i forget now which one or dirwction..but you will notice as the guage adjusts. when you recheck it make sure it mimics the natural position it would have sitting in the tank. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Sndfr||11-29-2000||08:08 PM||||I've heard that you can fill the tank up to full, take off the fuel gauge needle, and glue or reattach it at the full position, that simple.<P>I read it somewhere on this board so don't take it as fact, I'm not sure of if that works or not.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"><P>Just looking for re-assurance.<P>------------------<BR>-<B>matthew d.</B><BR><I>"who's yo daddy?"</I><P>'84 SE||||reg||3
Z||000004||GarethWright||11-29-2000||08:16 PM||||adjusting the needle itself would still give you the same range of motion as it was to begin with.  IE: if it shows 3/4 when full, and you move the needle to 4/4 when full, then it will read 1/4 when empty.  Adjusting the sender unit can be time-consuming, but you should get better results.||||reg||1
